0:{"buildId":"tVCS64WBwoyya1jLnGJF8","data":[{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","$L2",null,{"blog":{"id":"30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173","title":"Test Blog - Comfortable Rental Interiors","summary":"A second published test article for validating image upload, blog cards, and single blog content layout.","content":"This test post uses an interior image from the app assets so you can confirm uploaded featured images appear consistently across the blog list and detail view.","featuredImageUrl":"uploads/blogs/featured/afeace89-2a3d-43bf-89a8-ce80e39d13f2.webp","isPublished":true,"viewCount":185,"displayOrder":102,"createdAtUtc":"2026-08-22T13:55:25.8436269","updatedAtUtc":"2026-09-23T14:01:41.6334658Z","blogSections":[{"id":"e2450213-4b35-430f-d881-08df0055af57","blogId":"30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173","title":"Designing a Living Room That Photographs Honestly","content":"A strong rental interior starts with a living room that feels open, useful, and honest in photos. Natural light, clear walking paths, and enough seating for the listed capacity help guests imagine the stay without confusion. The best layouts avoid clutter but still feel lived in, using texture, lamps, rugs, and a few warm details to make the space inviting. For test content, this section is useful because it gives the blog page a longer paragraph beside a feature image and helps reveal whether line height, image crop, and content width feel balanced.","imageUrl":"uploads/blogs/30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173/sections/86d69791-cb18-48c4-b45a-ef0e72f1806a.webp","displayOrder":1,"sectionType":"Interior design"},{"id":"f823c6bc-997d-4f2a-d880-08df0055af57","blogId":"30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173","title":"Kitchen Details Guests Notice Quickly","content":"Guests judge comfort through small practical details, and the kitchen is usually where those details become obvious. A rental does not need a show kitchen to perform well, but it should have clean counters, enough storage, reliable appliances, basic cookware, and a simple place to prepare coffee or breakfast. Photos should show the layout clearly so guests know whether the space suits a short weekend, a family stay, or a longer remote-work visit. This makes kitchen imagery especially useful for testing how blog sections handle smaller interior photos and dense copy.","imageUrl":"uploads/blogs/30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173/sections/7eb4efe6-1ade-4ae9-a55a-42aaf0a11dda.webp","displayOrder":2,"sectionType":"Guest checklist"},{"id":"a659737c-428a-4605-d882-08df0055af57","blogId":"30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173","title":"Bedroom Comfort Is More Than a Bed Count","content":"Bedroom comfort is one of the easiest areas to underestimate. A clear listing should show the bed setup, storage, bedside lighting, privacy, and how the room connects to bathrooms or shared spaces. Guests also care about quiet, curtains, air conditioning, and enough room to move with luggage. For families, the difference between a twin room, queen room, sofa bed, or extra sleeping arrangement can decide whether the property is truly suitable. This section helps test repeated blog blocks and gives the page content that feels closer to a real rental advice article.","imageUrl":"uploads/blogs/30a8c2c3-4b16-41e5-e882-08df00550173/sections/17a85998-052d-4d9b-b54f-c88da6ba5f88.webp","displayOrder":3,"sectionType":"Comfort guide"}]},"relatedBlogs":[{"id":"479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173","title":"Test Blog - Red Sea Rental Guide1","summary":"A short test article for checking blog cards, detail routes, and featured images with a Red Sea location visual.","content":"Use this test post to verify that published blogs render on the public blog listing, open on the single blog page, and show uploaded featured imagery from the application assets.","featuredImageUrl":"uploads/blogs/featured/0e374218-7cb4-40cb-b194-f63be650eeec.webp","isPublished":true,"viewCount":160,"displayOrder":101,"createdAtUtc":"2026-08-22T13:55:20.6643648","updatedAtUtc":"2026-09-23T10:21:00.8773744","blogSections":[{"id":"5800889b-c3dd-4bfc-d87d-08df0055af57","blogId":"479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173","title":"Choosing the Right Red Sea Neighborhood","content":"The Red Sea coast has several rental personalities, and the right choice depends on the rhythm of the stay. El Gouna suits guests who want walkable marinas, restaurants, lagoons, and a polished resort feeling. Hurghada works well for travelers who want easier access to daily services, beaches, transport, and a wider range of budgets. Sahl Hasheesh and Makadi Heights are calmer choices for families and longer stays where privacy, views, and resort-style planning matter more than being close to every nightlife option. A useful first step is to decide whether the guest needs convenience, quiet, beach access, or space, then shortlist properties from there.","imageUrl":"uploads/blogs/479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173/sections/ea41df7a-6d21-4cc4-a0a4-28856c4f623b.webp","displayOrder":1,"sectionType":"Location guide"},{"id":"05d0ad17-9fec-480c-d87f-08df0055af57","blogId":"479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173","title":"How to Read a Rental Listing Before Booking","content":"Good rental decisions come from reading the details carefully, not only looking at the hero photo. Start with the bedroom count, bathroom count, capacity, and whether the property is available for the exact stay dates. Then compare the base price with seasonal pricing and any booking rules. Amenities should be treated as practical promises: pool view, sea view, private entrance, family friendly features, parking, and kitchen access all change the guest experience. The best listings make arrival simple by showing clear location details, cancellation rules, check-in timing, and contact expectations before payment.","imageUrl":"uploads/blogs/479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173/sections/10a8cb70-c3a1-4e1b-a5d1-42dddfbd8caf.webp","displayOrder":2,"sectionType":"Booking checklist"},{"id":"912aaae1-3d7e-48b3-d87e-08df0055af57","blogId":"479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173","title":"What Guests Should Confirm Before Arrival","content":"Before arrival, guests should confirm the pickup or entry process, the expected check-in window, and the best phone number for the local contact. It is also worth asking about nearby markets, beach access, parking instructions, and whether the property has any community rules that affect noise, visitors, or pool hours. For longer stays, the practical questions matter even more: laundry, kitchen equipment, Wi-Fi reliability, workspace comfort, and cleaning arrangements. Clear pre-arrival communication turns a rental from a listing into a smooth stay.","imageUrl":"uploads/blogs/479b63b2-eb1b-4e43-e881-08df00550173/sections/e4a55ab6-6724-4a0a-89cc-8ed83147afaa.webp","displayOrder":3,"sectionType":"Guest preparation"}]}]}],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":"$@5","staleTime":"$6","varyParams":null},{"rsc":"$L7","isPartial":"$@8","staleTime":"$6","varyParams":null},{"rsc":"$L9","isPartial":"$@a","staleTime":"$6","varyParams":"$b"},{"rsc":"$Lc","isPartial":"$@d","staleTime":"$6","varyParams":"$b"},{"rsc":"$Le","isPartial":"$@f","staleTime":"$6","varyParams":"$b"}],"isUpgradeableISRFallback":false,"a":"$@10","rootVaryParams":null,"needsRuntimeRequest":"$@11"}
